From 23d69df27015448b7810da0e7243c6ea4bd0818f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 05:46:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] lifecycle guard: close startup-file and keyword-led
 subshell fail-opens from review 5882059941

1. Startup files / prompt hooks (review finding 1). A stdin shell whose flags include -i, -l or
   --login sources $HOME startup files and runs PROMPT_COMMAND/PS1 before each line, and either
   can rewrite the stdin script (`exec 0< <(sed ...)`). Those flags (and -H, -k) no longer
   qualify a verbatim stdin shell, alone or combined (`-els`). HOME, ZDOTDIR, PROMPT_COMMAND,
   PS0-PS9, HISTFILE and INPUTRC are reserved prefix assignments. zsh leaves _STDIN_SHELLS: it
   sources $ZDOTDIR/.zshenv on every start, even non-interactive.

2. Keyword-led subshells (review finding 2). `function NAME ( ... )` is a keyword-led prefix, so
   its body is a subshell, not call arguments. Any walked text that may define or enable an alias
   (`alias`, `BASH_ALIASES`, `expand_aliases`, quote-split `al''ias`, ANSI-C `$'...'`) sets a
   walk-wide flag; call-argument paths are then judged as executed, so `alias T=' '; T ( bash
   big.sh )`, also when the alias comes from a sourced script, keeps main's scan-cap refusal.

Tests: 30 new rows (real Bash fake-hermes marker rows for every shape in the review), 28 RED on
8b2369e950, 224/224 GREEN; 6/6 mutants killed.
